The user is asking me to find a bug in the TypeScript debounce module and fix it. Let me analyze the code:

```typescript
export function debounce(fn: (...args: any[]) => void, waitMs: number) {
    let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;
    return (...args: any[]) => {
        if (timer !== null) {
            return; // already scheduled
        }
        timer = setTimeout(() => fn(args), waitMs);
        timer = null;
    };
}
```

The bug: `timer = setTimeout(() => fn(args), waitMs); timer = null;`

The problem is that the callback passed to setTimeout captures `args` by reference. When the timer fires, it executes `fn(args)`, but `args` is the current arguments at the time the timeout was scheduled. If a new call comes in before the timeout fires, `args` will be updated to the new arguments, and the timer will still execute `fn` with the old (stale) arguments.

The correct approach is to capture the arguments inside the timeout callback:

```typescript
timer = setTimeout(() => {
    fn(...args);
}, waitMs);
```

This way, when the timeout fires, it calls fn with the arguments at the time the timeout was scheduled (captured by closure).

